\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qc\b\f0\fs24\lang9 What is a Linked List\b0\fs22\line\line A Linked List is a linear data structure where each element (called a node) stores two things:\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The actual data\par
A pointer to the next node in memory\line\line Unlike arrays, data in a linked list is not stored in continuous memory.\par
Instead, each node is stored anywhere in RAM (scattered locations).\line\line     Each node has:\par

\pard{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf4\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1 data (value)\par
{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}a pointer to the next node (address)\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The whole linked list starts from a single pointer called head\par
(head stores the address of the first node)\line\line\line\b struct Node \{\par
    int data;\par
    Node* next;   // pointer to next node\par
\};\line\b0\line\b\fs24 Why Use a Linked List?\par
\b0\fs22 Your reason is 100% correct \f1\emdash  array ka size \f0\lang1033 does not change in runtime\f1\lang9 . But let me add more:\par
Array problems that Linked List solves:\par
Arrays allocate a fixed block of contiguous memory at creation. If you declare int arr[20], you get exactly 20 slots \emdash  no more, no less. To "resize", you'd have to allocate a completely new array, copy everything, and delete the old one. That's expensive.\par
Also, insertion and deletion in the middle of an array is O(n) because you have to shift all elements after the insertion point. In a linked list, once you have the correct pointer, insertion/deletion is just pointer manipulation \emdash  O(1).\line\line\f2\u-10179?\u-8905?\f0  Where to Use Linked Lists\par
When the size is not known at compile time, and dynamic memory allocation is needed\par
When there are frequent insertions and deletions, especially at the beginning or in the middle\par
To implement other data structures like Stacks, Queues, Graphs, and internal parts of Hash Tables\par
In memory-efficient systems where pre-allocating a fixed size would waste memory\par
In real-world applications like music players and browser history, where you need to move forward and backward (doubly linked list)\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qc In operating systems, especially for process scheduling (ready queue)\line\line\f2\u-10179?\u-8905?\f0  Array vs Vector vs Linked List (Final Comparison Chart)\line\line | Feature             | Array                 | Vector                 | Linked List                        |\par
| ------------------- | --------------------- | ---------------------- | ---------------------------------- |\par
| Size                | Fixed at compile time | Dynamic (auto-resizes) | Dynamic                            |\par
| Memory              | Contiguous            | Contiguous             | Non-contiguous (scattered)         |\par
| Access by index     | O(1)                  | O(1)                   | O(n)                               |\par
| Insert at beginning | O(n)                  | O(n)                   | O(1)                               |\par
| Insert at end       | O(1)                  | O(1) amortized (0(n) rare so avg is 0(1))         | O(n) *(or O(1) \line\line with tail pointer)* |\par
| Insert in middle    | O(n)                  | O(n)                   | O(n) search + O(1) insert          |\par
| Delete              | O(n)                  | O(n)                   | O(n) search + O(1) delete          |\par
| Cache performance   | Excellent             | Excellent              | Poor (pointer chasing)             |\par
| Extra memory        | None                  | None                   | Extra pointer per node             |\line\line\line Linked List does not give fast random access. Instead, it is good for changing size easily and inserting elements quickly at the front.\line\line\cf1\fs28 Important\line\line\line\cf0\fs22 Pehle ek cheez clear karo\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Vector already dynamic hai \f1\emdash  wo khud resize ho jata hai. Toh phir linked list kyun?\par
Answer: Size change karna sirf ek reason tha. Asli reasons different hain.\par
\par
Real Problem 1 \emdash  Insertion in the Middle\par
Socho tumhare paas ek waiting list hai \emdash  1000 log line mein khare hain ek concert ke liye.\par
[Ali] [Sara] [Ahmed] [Zara] [Bilal] ... 997 more\par
Ab ek VIP guest aaya \emdash  usse position 2 pe daalna hai (Sara ke baad).\par
Array/Vector mein kya hoga:\par
Step 1: Sara ke baad space banana parta hai\par
Step 2: Ahmed ko ek jagah aage shift karo\par
Step 3: Zara ko ek jagah aage shift karo\par
Step 4: Bilal ko ek jagah aage shift karo\par
... 998 logon ko shift karo\par
Step 999: Ab VIP ko position 2 pe daalo\par
998 shift operations \emdash  sirf ek insert ke liye. 1 million log hote toh 999,999 shifts.\par
\b Linked List mein kya hoga:\b0\par
Step 1: Sara ka next pointer VIP pe point karo\par
Step 2: VIP ka next pointer Ahmed pe point karo\par
Done. 2 pointer changes. Koi shift nahi.\par
Yeh O(1) hai agar tumhare paas already woh position ka pointer hai.\par
\b\par
Real Problem 2 \emdash  Deletion in the Middle\par
\b0 Same waiting list. Ahmed ko nikalna hai (beech se).\par
Array/Vector:\par
Ahmed nikala \f3\u8594?\f0  uske baad saare 997 log ek ek step peeche shift karo\par
997 operations.\par
Linked List:\par
Sara ka next \f3\u8594?\f0  Ahmed ke next (Zara) pe point karo\par
Ahmed delete karo\par
Done. 2 operations.\par
\par
\b Real Problem 3 \f1\emdash  Memory Allocation\par
\b0 Array/Vector ka problem:\par
Vector jab resize hota hai, wo yeh karta hai internally:\par
1. Nayi memory dhoondo \emdash  double size ki (e.g., 1000 se 2000)\par
2. Purana saara data copy karo nayi jagah pe\par
3. Purani memory free karo\par
Yeh copy operation O(n) hai. Aur isse bhi bada masla:\par
Vector ko contiguous (lagi hui) memory chahiye. Agar tumhare paas 1GB RAM free hai lekin woh scattered hai \emdash  100MB yahan, 200MB wahan \emdash  aur vector ko 500MB ka ek block chahiye, toh vector fail ho jata hai even though technically enough RAM hai.\par
RAM mein free space:\par
[__100MB__] [used] [__200MB__] [used] [__150MB__] [used] [__80MB__]\par
\par
Vector needs: 500MB contiguous block\par
Result: FAIL \emdash  allocation error\par
\par
Linked List needs: 500MB total, scattered anywhere\par

